.\"	@(#)fseek.3s	6.3 (Berkeley) 2/24/86
.\"
.TH FSEEK 3S  "February 24, 1986"
.AT 3
.SH NAME
fseek, fseeko, ftell, ftello, rewind \- reposition a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int fseek(FILE *" stream ", long " offset ", int " whence ");"
.PP
.BI "int fseeko(FILE *" stream ", off_t " offset ", int " whence ");"
.PP
.BI "long ftell(FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "off_t ftello(FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "void rewind(FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fseek
sets the position of the next input or output
operation on the
.IR stream .
The new position is at the signed distance
.I offset
bytes
from the beginning, the current position, or the end of the file,
according as 
.I whence
is
.BR SEEK_SET ,
.BR SEEK_CUR ,
or
.BR SEEK_END .
.PP
.B fseeko
is the large-file interface.  Its
.I off_t
argument is 64 bits on RetroBSD and can address offsets above 4 GiB.
.B fseek
is the source-compatible interface whose offset is a 32-bit
.IR long .
.PP
.I Fseek
undoes any effects of
.IR  ungetc (3S).
.PP
.I Ftell
returns the current value of the offset relative to the beginning
of the file associated with the named
.IR stream .
It is measured in bytes on UNIX;
on some other systems it is a magic cookie,
and the only foolproof way to obtain an 
.I offset
for
.IR fseek .
.PP
.B ftello
returns the same position as a 64-bit
.IR off_t .
.PP
.IR Rewind ( stream )
is equivalent to
.IR fseek ( stream ,
0L, 0).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
lseek(2),
fopen(3S)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B fseek
and
.B fseeko
return \-1 for improper seeks and zero on success.
.B ftell
and
.B ftello
return \-1 on error.
If the position does not fit in a
.IR long ,
.B ftell
fails with
.BR EOVERFLOW ;
.B ftello
still returns the full offset.
